  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Pick <strong>Orange</strong> if you want calls and texts included as a standard carrier service across Europe and don't mind paying more for it. Pick <strong>SimLocal</strong> if you want a real local phone number bundled into a UK eSIM at a much cheaper price, plus a working 10% discount code. They're not really competing for the same traveler: one is a telecom carrier's bundle, the other is a budget specialist with a narrower but more concrete number-based perk.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: Carrier Calling Bundle vs. a Bundled Local Number</h2>
  <p>
    Orange Travel is the international eSIM arm of Orange, one of Europe's largest telecom groups. Its Europe plans include unlimited calls and SMS plus hotspot tethering as standard, across 39 specifically named countries and territories on the plan we checked. That's a calling <em>service</em> layered on top of data, not a dedicated phone number.
  </p>
  <p>
    SimLocal works differently and more narrowly: most of its UK eSIM plans come bundled with an actual UK phone number, not just data or a generic calling allowance. We bought a UK plan ourselves to confirm it. That's a real number you can give out and receive calls and texts on, but it's specific to UK plans, not a Europe-wide calling feature baked into every destination the way Orange's bundle is.
  </p>

