  <h2>Two Things Worth Knowing Before You Decide</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Orange's "discount" isn't a code at all.</strong> We checked its checkout flow directly and found no promo field anywhere. The savings on bigger Europe plans are automatic strike-through markdowns, up to 40% off list price on the largest 90-day/500GB tier we checked, scaling with how much you commit to upfront.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>SimLocal's phone number is UK-specific, not global.</strong> If your trip covers multiple European countries the way Orange's calling bundle does, SimLocal's number perk doesn't follow you the same way; it's tied to its UK plans. For a broader calls/SMS bundle across the continent, Orange's carrier-backed approach covers more ground, just at a higher price and without a code to apply.
  </p>
  <p>
    For device setup and eSIM basics that apply to both providers, see our <a href="/compatible-devices/">eSIM compatible devices</a> guide.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2>Orange vs SimLocal FAQ</h2>
  <div class="content-faq">
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is cheaper, Orange or SimLocal</h3>
      <p>SimLocal, by a wide margin. Orange sits in our "Expensive" price bracket; SimLocal is "Cheap." Orange's higher price buys a carrier-backed calls/SMS/hotspot bundle across Europe, not just data.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Does either have a working discount code right now</h3>
      <p>SimLocal does: our own code gives 10% off, confirmed by buying a plan with it. Orange has no promo code field in its checkout at all; its only savings are automatic markdowns on bigger plans.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which one gives me a real phone number</h3>
      <p>SimLocal, but only on its UK plans, where most plans bundle an actual UK number for calls and texts. Orange includes unlimited calls and SMS as a standard feature across its European plans instead of a dedicated number.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>What if I need to cancel or get a refund</h3>
      <p>Both offer one. Orange advertises a full refund on an unused eSIM. SimLocal runs a "No Quibble Refunds" policy with no reason required.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

